A listing holds every picture of its subject, with a separate form for each appearance the film must keep consistent, so name only what the telling returns to: a person, place or thing the film must show more than once — again in a later chapter, or more than once across the shots of the chapter it lives in. A passer-by, a room one shot passes through, an object a single shot holds and the telling never comes back to is drawn by that shot and its words, and earns no entry of its own: leave it out of all three lists, however sharply the telling draws it.
Describe each one as a picture would have to know it. For a character, only what a drawing can show: state apparent age, build, facial features and distinguishing marks, hair colour, length and style, upper garment, lower garment, socks and footwear, and every accessory and item worn or carried, with exact colours, materials, shapes and details; say explicitly when nothing is worn or carried in a category. For a scene, give its exact layout, size, fixed furnishings, materials, light and one static state. For a prop, give its exact shape, dimensions, colour, material, markings and wear in one static state. A character's entry is a look and nothing else: no voice or way of speaking, no habits, mood or manner, no relationships, no backstory, and nothing that happens in the telling.
Every default description and every form description must be fully self-contained, static, precise and unambiguous. Repeat all shared identity and appearance details in every form; never refer to another form or say "same as before". Never put alternatives or changes over time into one description: do not use "或", "或者", "or", "either", "前期/后期", "early/late", "before/after", conditional wording, or a choice of outfits, ages, hairstyles, lighting or physical states. When the telling leaves a visible detail open, settle it as one concrete design consistent with the telling and reuse that exact detail wherever the subject appears; do not offer options and do not invent new people, elements, events or conflicting story facts.
The top-level description is the earliest appearance and is the default form. Put every later visually incompatible appearance in forms, under a short name based on a visible feature rather than a time label. Complementary details of the same appearance belong together in one description; a different outfit, hairstyle, age, damaged or intact state, weather, time of day, or lighting that changes what a drawing must show is a separate form of the same element, not a second element and not alternatives inside one description. Use an empty forms list when the appearance never changes.
Each character is drawn on its own, one person at a time, so a character is one person and its description describes that one person: a couple, a pair, a family or any other two or more the telling follows are that many characters, each listed and described on their own. A crowd, a queue or any other group the telling never follows person by person is not a character — keep it out of the cast, and let the scene it stands in say it is there.
Each scene is drawn on its own as the place a shot stands in, one place to an entry, and an entry is one concrete place rather than the telling's words for several places: two people each at their own workplace are two scenes, one for each place, and the one workplace they stand in together is still the one. A platform and the carriage it opens onto are two scenes. The same place at another hour or in other weather is still the one scene entry, but each visually different state is its own form with a complete static description.
Each prop is drawn on its own too, and only earns a listing where the story turns on it — what is handed over, hidden, found, carried or lost — and the telling comes back to it enough that its look has to stay. Light, shadow, weather, a moon, a sky and the ordinary fittings of a place are not props: they are the scene's light and state, and the scene's description says them. Leave out anything no shot would have to show, and anything only one shot shows.
